{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Module format: WHY this is NOT "commonjs"/"node16", even though the
    // compiled `main` entry (dist/extension.js, package.json "type":
    // "commonjs") loads as CommonJS via Node's require() in a real VS Code
    // extension host.
    //
    // SFE-P3c ("the run that adds a real build step") SOLVED the CommonJS-
    // vs-ESM problem the previous version of this comment deferred — here
    // is what actually happens, and why this compiler-option pairing is
    // STILL the correct one now that a real build exists (not merely left
    // over from before it did):
    //
    // This package's one load-bearing production import is
    // `@dimm-city/gutterpress-editor` (packages/editor), which is ESM-only
    // by design (package.json "type": "module") and exposes its surface
    // ONLY via a package.json "exports" map pointing directly at TypeScript
    // source — no "main", no "types" fallback (see packages/editor's own
    // src/index.ts header). `scripts/build.mjs` builds dist/extension.js
    // with `bun build --target=node --format=cjs --external vscode` —
    // "vscode" is the ONLY external specifier. `@dimm-city/gutterpress-editor`
    // is NOT external: Bun's bundler resolves its "exports" map, reads the
    // TypeScript source directly, and BUNDLES/INLINES it into the same
    // dist/extension.js output, converting its ESM syntax to the file's
    // CJS format as part of that one bundling pass. The "CommonJS `require()`
    // of a raw ESM file" problem this comment used to describe only applies
    // to NODE'S OWN native module loader trying to load a separate ESM
    // file at RUNTIME — it never arises here, because by the time Node
    // ever sees dist/extension.js, the dependency's code has already been
    // rewritten and concatenated into that one CJS file at BUILD time.
    // "vscode" stays external because it is not an npm-installable module
    // at all — the real extension host injects it — so there is nothing
    // for the bundler to inline; Node's own `require("vscode")` resolves it
    // against the host's own module registry, exactly as every real VS
    // Code extension expects.
    //
    // Given that, this file's job is unchanged from before the build step
    // existed: TYPECHECK the real dependency edge (`import { EDITOR_PROTOCOL_VERSION }
    // from "@dimm-city/gutterpress-editor/core"`, now consumed by
    // src/protocol/**, src/host/**, and src/provider.ts, not just proven as
    // an unused import). Empirically verified against this repo's exact
    // pinned `typescript` (5.9.3, see root bun.lock) before choosing this
    // pairing (verification preserved from the original investigation):
    //   - "moduleResolution": "node10"/"node" CANNOT see a package's
    //     "exports" map at all — tsc reports the module's real path but
    //     refuses it: "this result could not be resolved under your
    //     current 'moduleResolution' setting. Consider updating to
    //     'node16', 'nodenext', or 'bundler'" (TS2307).
    //   - "module": "node16" (the only module setting "moduleResolution":
    //     "node16" accepts — TS5110 rejects any other pairing) DOES see
    //     the export, but then correctly refuses it for a different
    //     reason: a CommonJS-mode file statically importing an ESM-only
    //     package cannot become a `require()` call (TS1479 — "the
    //     referenced file is an ECMAScript module and cannot be imported
    //     with 'require'. Consider writing a dynamic 'import(...)' call
    //     instead"). This is TypeScript correctly describing what NODE
    //     ITSELF would refuse at runtime WITHOUT a bundler in the loop —
    //     exactly the case this package's real build step avoids by
    //     bundling instead of relying on Node's native loader.
    //   - "moduleResolution": "bundler" flatly REJECTS "module": "commonjs"
    //     under this exact TypeScript version (TS5095 — "'bundler' can
    //     only be used when 'module' is set to 'preserve' or to 'es2015'
    //     or later").
    // "module": "preserve" + "moduleResolution": "bundler" remains the only
    // combination that typechecks this real dependency edge, and it
    // matches both packages/cli's and packages/editor's own tsconfig
    // pairing, and — not coincidentally — the actual bundler
    // (`scripts/build.mjs`'s `bun build`) that resolves it for real at
    // build time.
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "moduleDetection": "force",

    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    // The extension host is a Node process (D9: "VS Code host owns...
    // file and workspace events"), so Node builtins are permitted in this
    // package — unlike packages/editor (D4: framework-free, browser-safe,
    // "no ... node:fs ... imports"). src/provider.ts uses "node:crypto"
    // for its webview CSP nonce.
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": false
  },
  // Mirrors packages/editor/tsconfig.json: tests/ is part of the strict
  // typecheck program (this is a brand-new package with no other layer
  // that would otherwise catch a type error in a test file, and the run's
  // gate gives `typecheck` and `test` separate, equally load-bearing
  // commands).
  "include": ["src", "tests"]
}
